qsm-pve-direct for Proxmox VE 9
===============================

`qsm-pve-direct` is a browser-native PVE console transport. Install it as the
sole non-stock console transport on the node.

It uses PVE's existing authenticated Web UI session and `VM.Console` ACL.
The browser sends its WebRTC SDP offer to the node-local signalling service
(`qsm-pve-direct-signal`, TLS on TCP 8007 with the node certificate), which
confirms the browser's PVE ticket and `VM.Console` with pveproxy's own
`/access/ticket`; the node-local terminal service answers it and starts one
QEMU Display1 worker.  Negotiated H.264 or HEVC video and Opus remain
encoded until the browser's WebRTC implementation. There is no pairing PIN,
native client installer or host desktop server; the only listening port is
8007 (plus one UDP port per open console for WebRTC).

Install:

    sudo apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_*.deb
    sudo systemctl enable --now qsm-pve-direct-terminal.service qsm-pve-direct-signal.service

The PVE web page gets one script tag (a diverted `index.html.tpl`) only for
the `pve-manager` versions and template checksums the package knows; on any
other version the stock page stays and VMs keep the noVNC console until a
package update adds it.  `qsm-pve-direct-ui status` shows the state.

The service reads PVE's saved VM configuration and creates the private
Display1 bus before the VM is started. No per-VM policy file is required: the
portable `auto` policy prefers HEVC when the current browser offers H.265 and
a tested NVENC/QSV/VA-API HEVC encoder is available, otherwise the H.264 lane
(NVENC, QSV, VA-API, then `libx264`).
After saving a Display1 setting, the service
reconciles the bus automatically. Package upgrades may restart the terminal
service, but preserve every live private Display1 bus: a running configured
VM does not need to be rebooted solely for this package update.

In the PVE VM's **Hardware → Display → Advanced**, enable **QSM Display1**
and select one profile:

* **VirGL GPU (GL)** saves PVE Graphic card as `none`, then owns one private
  `virtio-vga-gl` device and `-display dbus,...,gl=on,rendernode=...`. PVE VNC
  cannot coexist with that GL Display1 backend.
* **CPU — Standard VGA, VirtIO or VMware (no GL)** keeps the selected PVE
  `std`, non-GL `virtio` or `vmware` graphic adapter and writes only
  `-display dbus,...,gl=off`. It needs neither a render node nor a host GPU;
  normal PVE VNC remains usable alongside QSM Direct.

**Audio** (on by default for a new console) adds an Intel HDA sound card on a
QEMU dbus audiodev (`-audiodev dbus,id=qsm-direct-audio,...`, named in
`-display ...,audiodev=qsm-direct-audio`).  The console starts muted, as
browsers require; its speaker button turns the sound on.

Reopening the dialog restores the checkbox, profile, and (for VirGL only)
render node from the saved arguments. The existing VM **Console** entry in the
left navigation (between **Summary** and **Hardware**) opens QSM Direct only
for one of those exact private Display1 configurations; otherwise it remains
the stock noVNC Console. QSM Direct renders in that same PVE Console area.
The QSM Direct entry in the top Console split menu remains an optional
separate-window launch and is enabled under the same condition. Start the VM,
then choose **Console**.
Resizing the console window or
entering/leaving fullscreen sends the selected even viewport to QEMU Display1
and causes a fresh video configuration/IDR boundary when the guest adapter
implements `Console.SetUIInfo`. Non-GL VirtIO does; QEMU Standard VGA exposes
a fixed Display1 scanout and rejects that optional method, so QSM keeps the
video and input connected at its guest-selected mode rather than failing the
console.

The Console window's **Settings** panel stores the media policy per VM on the
node in root-owned `/etc/qsm-pve-direct/instances.d/<VMID>.conf` (0600), not
in browser storage or unsupported `qm.conf` keys. Its choices are
**Automatic** (prefer negotiated hardware HEVC, otherwise probe H.264 with
`libx264` fallback), **Hardware only**
(fail clearly rather than consume CPU when no accelerator works), and
**Software** (`libx264`). Close all QSM Direct windows for the VM and reopen
one after saving: one worker is shared by its active viewers.

HEVC is negotiated when the current browser SDP offers H.265 and the node
initializes a hardware HEVC encoder; the answer repeats the browser's own
H265 payload type and profile/tier/level. Qualified against Chrome on Apple
silicon with `hevc_nvenc`.
This is a browser/WebRTC and server-RTP capability boundary, not a VirGL
limitation. A VM shares one worker, so mixed viewers must support its active
codec or reconnect after the existing Console is closed.

## Performance profile compared with PVE noVNC

This is a transport comparison, not an invented universal millisecond
benchmark: the result depends on guest workload, network loss, browser
decoder, chosen encoder, and display profile. Compare both consoles at the
same guest resolution and refresh rate. QSM's release stress suite records
first-video time, Chrome jitter-buffer delay, key-to-pixel latency, hover
latency, and continuous-drag gaps; noVNC has no equivalent built-in PVE
telemetry, so its numbers must be collected with the same guest fixture before
publishing a hardware-specific result.

| Performance property | QSM Direct | Stock PVE noVNC |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser video path | WebRTC H.264 or negotiated hardware HEVC / Opus; the browser may use its hardware decoder | RFB rectangles over a reliable WebSocket, decoded and painted by noVNC/Canvas |
| Host work for changing pixels | One low-latency H.264 or HEVC encode per VM worker, shared by its viewers; hardware encoders are preferred by `Automatic` | QEMU VNC/RFB rectangle updates; no video encoder or browser video-decoder path |
| Pointer behaviour under congestion | Latest pointer samples use an unordered, non-retransmitted WebRTC channel, so stale motion is discarded | Pointer events share the ordered reliable WebSocket stream; a delayed stream cannot skip earlier events |
| Video under packet loss | Media can recover at the next video intra frame while input keeps its separate control path | TCP/WebSocket head-of-line recovery delays later RFB updates behind a missing packet |
| Resize | One even Display1 mode request followed by a video configuration/IDR boundary | Rescales or redraws the current RFB framebuffer; it does not request a QEMU Display1 guest mode |
| Best fit | Interactive desktop, animation, video, and high-latency or lossy links | Firmware text console, installation, recovery, and a zero-extra-transport baseline |

For a reproducible QSM result, run the documented
`run-qsm-direct-stress-suite.py` from the PVE node and retain its single JSON
record with the browser/guest topology. Do not compare its result with a
noVNC value gathered at a different guest geometry, browser, or network path.

An administrator may still create a root-owned policy file directly to pin a
specific node encoder without changing the display setup:

    QSM_DIRECT_QEMU_DBUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/qsm-pve-direct/<VMID>/qemu-display1.bus
    QSM_DIRECT_ENCODER=h264_nvenc

To pin HEVC, pair a hardware encoder with `QSM_DIRECT_CODEC=hevc`, for example:

    QSM_DIRECT_CODEC=hevc
    QSM_DIRECT_ENCODER=hevc_nvenc

For VA-API additionally set
`QSM_DIRECT_VAAPI_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128`.

The direct WebRTC console provides video, guest audio (with **Audio**
enabled), keyboard, pointer, scrolling, dynamic Display1 resolution, and
optional guest clipboard integration. For clipboard install the matching `qsm-desktop-agent` package
inside the Linux VM, then run `sudo qsm-desktop-agent-setup USER` once for its
logged-in desktop user. On Wayland, also enable `qsm-desktop-clipboard.service`
in that user's session.

The browser's **Copy** button copies the guest's current plain-text selection
to the local system clipboard; `Ctrl/Cmd+C` first sends `Ctrl+C` to the focused
Linux guest application, then copies the resulting selection. **Paste** and
`Ctrl/Cmd+V` read the browser clipboard, update the guest selection, then send
`Shift+Insert` to the focused guest application. Browser security requires this
explicit user gesture; a guest clipboard update cannot silently overwrite the
host system clipboard.

LXC container consoles
----------------------

A container has no QEMU display.  QSM Direct runs the container's desktop
session (KDE Plasma, Wayland or X11) inside a headless sway ("the display")
in the container; the host media worker captures that display (wlr-screencopy)
and injects input (virtual pointer/keyboard), using the host GPU encoder.
There is no audio from a container yet.
The worker resolves the display socket inside the container's root (symlinks
cannot redirect it to a host socket) and drops to "nobody" once connected.

The container console is the package qsm-pve-direct-lxc (the setup tool); the
container side is the package qsm-console-guest, which the tool installs into
the container with apt.

Enable a running Debian/Ubuntu container that already has a Plasma desktop:

    qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable <vmid> [--session wayland|x11] [--grace SECONDS] [--restart]

This passes the GPU through (NVIDIA device nodes and the DRM render node;
--restart applies it), installs NVIDIA userspace matching the host kernel
module from the host's NVIDIA-Linux-x86_64-<version>.run (--nvidia-run FILE),
installs the container package qsm-console-guest with apt (pulling in sway,
xwayland, PyQt5, acl), which runs the container's consoles as system
services -- one per tty the container runs with (its "tty" option, PVE
default 2), like PVE's terminal console:

    qsm-consoles.service       at boot: starts one console per /dev/ttyN
    qsm-display@N.service      headless sway (user qsm-display), /run/qsm-display-N
    qsm-seat-keeper@N.service  keeps the display's input devices stable
    qsm-login@N.service        login manager: login screen, PAM, the session
    qsm-session-N.service      (transient) one logged-in desktop session

Every PVE Console gets its own console and starts at its login screen; no
Console ever shows another Console's desktop.  Like a tty in use, a console
belongs to the PVE user who opened it: when that Console is closed with a
desktop still logged in, the desktop waits --grace seconds (default 300) for
the same PVE user -- who then gets it back -- and is logged out after that.
A left login screen is freed at once.  When all consoles are in use, the
browser says so.  The count follows
the container's tty option by itself: like for the terminal console, a changed
value applies at the container's next start (host and container both read
the running value).  One container account can have one console desktop at a
time (two Plasma sessions of one user break each other).

A container has no VT, so greetd/SDDM cannot run (an enabled SDDM is disabled).
The login screen lists the container's accounts (new ones appear live); pick
one, enter its password, choose Plasma (Wayland) or Plasma (X11).  qsm-login
checks the password with PAM (service qsm-login) and starts the session
through systemd with PAMName=qsm-direct-session, i.e. a real logind session
on seat0 (polkit dialogs, user manager, limits work as after a normal
desktop login; with several desktops only the newest is "active" to polkit).
Log Out returns to the login screen.  The clipboard works without setup:
every desktop session runs qsm-clipboard-agent, which bridges Klipper (the
same on Wayland and X11) to the console over a socket in
/run/qsm-login/clip-N that only the logged-in user can reach.  Only the current client of a console's
display -- its login screen or its logged-in user -- may connect to it (an
ACL on the display socket, like logind's uaccess).  The host asks a console's
login manager for its state or a logout over a root-only socket in the
container (/run/qsm-login/N/control), opened without following symlinks.

A desktop user "qsm" (--user/--uid) is created if missing and added to the
sudo group (polkit administrators; skip with --no-admin).  Any account of the
container with a password can log in; set one:

    pct exec <vmid> -- passwd qsm

enable writes /etc/qsm-pve-direct/containers.d/<vmid>.conf.  The container's
Console button then offers "QSM Direct" to users with VM.Console on
/vms/<vmid>; its Console tab keeps the stock xterm.js terminal.

    qsm-pve-direct-lxc session <vmid> wayland|x11   default session of the login screen
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc status  <vmid>
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc disable <vmid>

Notes: the in-session screen locker's auto-lock is disabled (console access is
already gated by PVE) and KScreen's automatic mode selection is off (the
console window decides the resolution).  X11 clients render through SHM: the
headless display offers no DRM device for Xwayland's glamor.
